Louis,
I've actually gone through the same thing as you do. But as you said, it happened again, so I needed to find a new way. Shortly I discovered that if you do not shut down the R session, but then using Session -> New Session actually works. I hope this works for you as well, maybe easier than installing every time.

Cheers,

On Wed, Sep 1, 2021 at 8:32 PM Louis Lindley <lindle...@gmail.com> wrote:

> Han,
>
> What ended up working for me was downloading R on my partners computer and then installing all the necessary packages. Once I did that on her computer I got the full output no problem! But then the next day when I ran it again (on her computer) I ran into the same issue. So I am not sure why this is happening, but if you need the results pronto I would recommend un-installing R, re-installing the packages, and then run your analysis. It is a band aid fix for sure, but one that worked.
>
> On Wednesday, September 1, 2021 at 8:50:31 AM UTC-5 S.Han wrote:
> > Hello,
> > I have the exact same problem (summary only shows "est.ave"). My Lavaan and SemTools packages are both up-to-date from github. So Lavaan version 0.6-10.1655 and SemTools 0.5-5.906. I saved the imputed data separately, re-boot R, checked sessionsInfo, load only SemTools but still I cannot obtain the whole data. I tried countless times but it remains the same.
> > Can anyone please help me? Thanks!
> > Best,
> > Han
> >
> >
> > > > now it is only giving me "est.ave":
> > >
> > > That is the default method for the lavaanList class, from which lavaan.mi inherits.  This suggests there is a problem with R searching the correct path to find the lavaan.mi methods.  Save your mice.imp object, e.g.,
> > >
> > > saveRDS(mice.imp, file = "impList.rds")
> > >
> > > Then open a fresh R session and load it, and load ONLY semTools (which already loads lavaan as a dependency, along with lavaan's dependencies).
> > >
> > > mice.imp <- saveRDS("impList.rds")
> > > library(semTools)
> > > fitscalar <- cfa.mi(CFA,estimator="wlsmv", data=mice.imp,group="trans", group.equal=c("loadings","intercepts"))
> > > fitMeasures(fitscalar, c("chisq", "df", "pvalue", "cfi", "rmsea"))
> > > summary(fitscalar)
> > >
> > > Does it work then?  If so, I would like to know what other packages were showing up in your sessionInfo(), which might have interfered with semTools.
> > >
> > > Terrence D. Jorgensen
> > > Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics
> > > Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam
> > > http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen
